Fixes AI-upscaled characters/objects visibly "floating above" native
scenery, furniture, and the right-click verb menu instead of being
correctly occluded by them.

Root cause: Screen::update()'s hires compositor blends three AI-upscaled
override layers (text tooltips, scene sprites/characters, cursor/inventory
sprites) on top of the nearest-neighbor-upscaled native composite,
unconditionally, in a fixed order, every frame - regardless of what the
native low-res framebuffer's final content at those same pixels ended up
being. Most bg-shape scenery (ordinary furniture, doorframes, etc.) has no
AI sprite override of its own (see
rosetattoo-hires-character-object-sprites.patch), so when such an
un-overridden shape - or the verb menu, or any other native-only UI - is
drawn *after* a character in native z-order (correctly occluding it in the
native-diff composite), nothing tells the AI scene-sprite layer that its
earlier-queued character pixels there are now supposed to be hidden again.
The AI layer blends on top regardless, making Holmes/Watson/NPCs appear to
hover in front of things that should be in front of them.

Fix: add per-native-pixel occlusion "provenance" tracking. When a
shape/character is queued into the AI scene-sprite layer (via
queueRoseTattooHiresSceneSprite(), immediately after that shape has already
been drawn into the native low-res _backBuffer1 by the caller), also record
what native pixel value _backBuffer1 held right at that moment for every
pixel the AI sprite covers
(_roseTattooHiresSceneSpriteExpectedNative/Valid). At final composite time,
blendRoseTattooHiresSceneSpriteLayer() compares this recorded "expected"
native value against the real final native framebuffer pixel (after every
later shape/widget in the frame has had its chance to draw) and skips
blending the AI-upscaled pixel wherever they differ - i.e. wherever
something else painted over that native spot afterward. This is
architecturally general: it fixes un-overridden-furniture occlusion, verb
menu occlusion, and other native-only UI occlusion simultaneously, without
per-widget special-casing. Only the scene-sprite layer opts into this
tracking (via the new trackNativeProvenance parameter on
blitRoseTattooHiresSpriteOverride()); the cursor/inventory sprite layer
(queueRoseTattooHiresSprite()) is unaffected since it's always meant to
render on top of everything.

Depends on rosetattoo-hires-character-object-sprites.patch (introduces
_roseTattooHiresSceneSpriteLayer, queueRoseTattooHiresSceneSprite(),
blendRoseTattooHiresSceneSpriteLayer(), clearRoseTattooHiresSceneSpriteLayer()).

Known limitation: this assumes shape/character draw coordinates are
already in the same coordinate space as the native screen buffer
(getPixels()), matching how the pre-existing scene-sprite-layer code
already treats these coordinates. This should hold for the common
non-scrolling-room case; wide scrolling rooms are unverified.

